    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, the historical king believed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Assyrian texts, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> at its peak from about 720 BCE to about 695 BCE by Eusebius or 676 BCE by Julius Africanus.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 715 BCE over the Neo-Hittite lands of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; an Assyrian inscription of 709 BCE suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> had offered submission and struck a truce.</li><li>Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> confirm a violent destruction around 675 BCE, and the so-called Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> held a wooden chamber under a vast tumulus with a coffin, furniture and food offerings.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and others, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> killed himself by drinking bulls' blood when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> overran his kingdom and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

