      <figure class="rf-chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 206" role="img" aria-label="Relative power of the Kingdom of Phrygia over time, peaking in 715 BCE"><line class="rf-base" x1="20" x2="740" y1="160" y2="160"/><path class="rf-area" d="M20.0 119.8 L340.0 93.0 L500.0 66.2 L596.0 39.4 L612.0 26.0 L631.2 39.4 L676.0 106.4 L740.0 146.6 L740.0 160 L20.0 160 Z"/><path class="rf-line" d="M20.0 119.8 L340.0 93.0 L500.0 66.2 L596.0 39.4 L612.0 26.0 L631.2 39.4 L676.0 106.4 L740.0 146.6"/><circle class="rf-peak" cx="612.0" cy="26.0" r="5"/><text class="rf-peak-label" x="612.0" y="16.0" text-anchor="middle">Peak 715 BCE</text><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="500.0" x2="500.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="500.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="500.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">1</text><title>8th century BCE: The kingdom with its capital at Gordium expands into an empire dominating most of central and western Anatolia, bordering Assyria and Urartu.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="596.0" x2="596.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="596.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="596.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">2</text><title>c. 720 BCE: Midas, the Mita of Assyrian texts, begins his reign over Phrygia at the peak of its power.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="612.0" x2="612.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="612.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="612.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">3</text><title>715 BCE: Phrygia enters military conflict with Assyria over the Neo-Hittite lands of central Anatolia.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="631.2" x2="631.2" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="631.2" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="631.2" y="164" text-anchor="middle">4</text><title>709 BCE: An Assyrian inscription from the reign of Sargon of Assyria names Mita, suggesting Phrygia has offered its submission and struck a truce.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="676.0" x2="676.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="676.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="676.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><title>c. 695 BCE: Cimmerian invaders sack Gordium; by Strabo's account Midas drinks bulls' blood and dies.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>c. 675 BCE: The destruction of Gordium confirmed by excavation marks the end of the independent kingdom; the Cimmerians remain in western Anatolia.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">900 BCE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">675 BCE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The kingdom with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> expands into an empire dominating most of central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Urartu.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 720 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Assyrian texts, begins his reign over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> at the peak of its power.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">715 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> enters military conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> over the Neo-Hittite lands of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">709 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">An Assyrian inscription from the reign of Sargon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a>, suggesting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> has offered its submission and struck a truce.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 695 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cimmerian invaders sack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a>; by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> drinks bulls' blood and dies.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 675 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> confirmed by excavation marks the end of the independent kingdom; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> remain in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gordion.jpg" alt="Gordion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gordion_Early_Phrygian_East_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gordion Archive, Penn Museum, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> occupied the western end of the high Anatolian plateau, an arid land of hot summers and cold winters where olives will not easily grow and the ground is given to grazing and barley. Its heart lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sangarius</a>, the Sakarya, and its tributary the Porsuk, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> near modern Eskişehir and the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> stood; to the south lay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> City (Yazılıkaya) and Synnada, and in the south-west the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> with Laodicea and Hierapolis. Greek tradition held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> had come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says they were called Bryges. Their language, known from inscriptions at Gordium, was Indo-European and not of the Anatolian family of Hittite; the modern consensus makes Greek its closest relative.</p><p>When they arrived is disputed. Some scholars treat the migration as a legend born of a chance likeness of names and place the Phrygians' entry just before or after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> at the start of the 12th century BCE, perhaps among the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>'; others believe they were already settled on the Sakarya in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> among the Hittites' western subjects. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> the Phrygians, 'the people of Otreus and godlike Mygdon', fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> from a homeland on the Sangarius. Legend makes Gordias, a farmer acclaimed king when he rode up to the oracle's temple in a cart, the refounder of Gordium, his ox-cart tied to its shaft with the Gordian Knot; his son, adopted in some versions, was the first Midas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/midas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midas.jpg" alt="Midas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-figure_stamnos_Silenos_Mida_440BC_Cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Midas Painter, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Phrygian economy rested on the plateau's livestock and barley, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> the land was famous for its wine and its 'brave and expert' horsemen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> sat on the old trackway through the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> that Darius later remade as the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> from Pessinus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a>, and the historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> is reported to have had good relations and close trade ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and to have married an Aeolian Greek princess. Under his rule a system of writing in Phrygian developed and flourished at Gordium, using an alphabet derived from the Phoenician and similar to the Greek, and a distinctive pottery, Polished Ware, appeared.</p><p>The kingdom's wealth is clearest in its tombs. The burial popularly called the Tomb of Midas is a wooden structure deeply buried under a vast tumulus, with grave goods, a coffin, furniture and food offerings, now in the Archaeological Museum in Ankara. The Gordium site shows a considerable building programme in the 6th century BCE under Lydian kings including the proverbially rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, and in Seleucid times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> settled 2,000 Jewish families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> with land, tax exemptions and vineyards. The Phrygian cap worn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in Greek art, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a>, survived into modern imagery as the liberty cap of the American and French revolutionaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 8th century BCE the kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> expanded into an empire dominating most of central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, bordering the larger and more powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> to the south-east and Urartu to the north-east. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Eusebius and Julius Africanus, is believed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Assyrian texts, king of the Mushki; scholars think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> Mushki because they were then campaigning in a joint army with that eastern Anatolian people against Assyria. From 715 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> fought Assyria for control of the Neo-Hittite lands of central Anatolia that Assyria regarded as its own territory. An Assyrian inscription of 709 BCE, in the reign of Sargon of Assyria, mentions Mita and suggests that Phrygia had offered its submission and struck a truce by then.</p><p>The kingdom was then overwhelmed by Cimmerian invaders, who sacked and destroyed Gordium; according to Strabo and others Midas committed suicide by drinking bulls' blood. Eusebius ends his reign about 695 BCE and Julius Africanus in 676 BCE, and excavation confirms a violent destruction of Gordium around 675 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> stayed in western Anatolia, tried and failed to encroach on Assyrian territory, and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, which expelled them by about 620 BCE and then took much of Phrygia as its eastern frontier, while Assyria absorbed some of the eastern Phrygian lands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cybele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cybele.jpg" alt="Cybele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cybele_Getty_Villa_57.AA.19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marshall Astor, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> was a kingdom whose rulers, Greek sources suggest, drew their authority from the cult of the Mother. In the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, adopted by the childless Gordias and taken under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a>, a Phrygian king acting as the goddess's visible representative could designate his successor. The Phrygian pantheon, unlike the Hittite and Luwian, was headed by a goddess, Matar Kubeleya, 'Mother of the Mountain Peaks', mistress of wild mountains and their animals, whose priests the Corybantes, 'head-shakers', served her in orgiastic rites of pipe, cymbal and ecstatic dance; her consort was Attis, her sacred mountain Agdistis. Beside her stood the storm god Tiws, the moon god Mas, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Men, and the goddess Artimis. The oracle of Sabazios, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>' to the Greeks, at Telmissus named Gordias king.</p><p>After the fall of the kingdom the land was governed by others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> made it its eastern frontier; from the 540s BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Lydia, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and after 521 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> set up the Phrygian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a>, with its capital at Dascylium, modern Ergili. Alexander cut the Gordian Knot in the temple of Sabazios at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> in 333 BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> overran the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> took the southern remnant in 188 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> inherited it in 133 BCE, splitting it between the provinces of Galatia and Asia and later, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, into Phrygia Salutaris and Phrygia Pacatiana.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>'s lasting gift to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> was religious and musical. The cult of Matar Kubeleya spread from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a> as Kybele, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a>, with Attis beside her. The earliest traditions of Greek music were held to derive from Phrygia through the Greek colonies of Anatolia, among them the Phrygian mode, counted the warlike mode, and the aulos, the reed instrument with two pipes; myth made Orpheus the music tutor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> tied Phrygian music to the ecstatic rites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, Cybele and Sabazios, though recent scholarship finds almost no archaeological evidence that Phrygia was the origin of ecstatic cults and reads the idea as a Greek and Roman invention that made a foreign, 'oriental' Phrygia their symbolic homeland.</p><p>Phrygia also entered the imagination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> through its kings: Gordias and the Gordian Knot that Alexander cut, Midas of the golden touch, Tantalus of Mount Sipylus, Mygdon who warred with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazons">Amazons</a>, and Hecabe, Priam's Phrygian queen. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Phrygia held prosperous Jewish communities, with synagogues at Acmonia, Synnada and Hierapolis; Paul and Silas preached there, and in the 2nd century AD the village of Ardabau gave rise to Montanism, 'the Phrygian heresy', with its ecstatic spirituality and women priests and its new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> at Pepouza.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croesus.jpg" alt="Croesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croesus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent kingdom ended in a single catastrophe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, the last independent king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, saw his realm overwhelmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, who sacked and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> around 695 BCE by one ancient reckoning; the destruction level found by the excavators is dated around 675 BCE. The Cimmerians remained in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> expelled them about 620 BCE, and Phrygia then became a Lydian province and the eastern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>' empire. In the 540s BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s conquest of Lydia made it Persian.</p><p>Gordium itself had one more moment, when Alexander passed through in 333 BCE and severed the Gordian Knot; in the chaos after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> overran northern Phrygia, which became Galatia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> captured and destroyed the old capital, which disappeared from history. The southern remnant passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> in 188 BCE and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE, and the land was divided and redivided as Roman and Byzantine provinces until the Turks overran it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 and took complete control in the 13th century. Over those centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> became Greek-speaking Christians and merged into the Byzantine state; their language lasted to the 6th century AD. The name Phrygia survived as a territorial designation until the last remnant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1453, and then passed out of use.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sangarius</a>, rose in the 8th century BCE to dominate central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, fought and then submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, and fell when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> sacked Gordium around 695–675 BCE; its cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a> and its musical mode outlived it.</em></p>

